"""Original pre-class reading; chapter numbers refer to the supplied TOC photos."""
READINGS = [
 dict(title='Reading a phase diagram before calculating',lab='index',book='15.1-15.4, 15.9-15.12',figure='vle',
 question='At fixed temperature, how does lowering pressure change a binary mixture?',
 concepts=[('The state and the constraints','Start by naming the components, phases and imposed conditions. For a TP flash, temperature T, pressure P and overall mole fraction z are given. The calculation finds the phase amounts and compositions. The liquid composition x, vapor composition y and overall composition z have different meanings. A bubble calculation supplies liquid composition; a dew calculation supplies vapor composition.'),('Reading P-x-y','At fixed T, the bubble curve bounds the liquid region and the dew curve bounds the vapor region. For the ideal example below, pressure above the bubble curve gives liquid and pressure below the dew curve gives vapor. A horizontal line through the two-phase region connects coexisting x and y. A vertical line at z follows changes in pressure at fixed overall composition. The position of z along a tie line determines phase amounts.'),('Equilibrium and amounts','Equal component fugacities establish phase equilibrium. A material balance supplies the amount information. For a nonreacting binary with two phases, the phase rule gives two intensive degrees of freedom; specifying T and P fixes the coexistence compositions in an ordinary two-phase region. Overall composition then determines amounts. Degenerate states, such as an azeotrope, need special care.')],
 equations=['P_bubble = Σ x_i P_i^sat;  1/P_dew = Σ y_i/P_i^sat  (ideal phases, fixed T)','z_i = (1 − β)x_i + βy_i;  β = (z_1 − x_1)/(y_1 − x_1)'],
 example='Use synthetic saturation pressures 150 and 60 kPa at 350 K, with z1=0.5 and P=95 kPa. The bubble and dew pressures evaluated at composition 0.5 are 105 and 85.7143 kPa. Thus 95 kPa lies inside the two-phase interval. The horizontal tie line gives x1=0.388889 and y1=0.614035. The lever rule gives β=0.493506. Substitution closes the component-1 balance to rounding. The second component must also balance.',
 check='At 110 kPa the same feed is liquid in this model. At 80 kPa it is vapor. Do not apply the two-phase lever rule outside the coexistence interval.',
 prompts=['Sketch where the bubble curve, dew curve and z=0.5 lie. What does a horizontal line tell you?', 'Why can the same numerical value 0.5 mean different inputs in bubble, dew and flash calculations?', 'Predict the phase at 110 kPa before opening Lab 01. Which quantities remain specified?'],
 optional='After the ideal case, compare modified Raoult predictions using a liquid activity model. Virial vapor corrections and an adiabatic energy balance are optional extensions in Labs 09 and 07. Cubic-EOS VLE is reference material, not an implemented lab solver.'),
 dict(title='From measurements to activities and fitted models',lab='vle-input',book='11.4, 11.8-11.11, 14.1-14.4, 14.8, 15.14-15.18',figure=None,
 question='What evidence makes an activity-model fit useful?',
 concepts=[('Activity has a reference','Chemical potential can be written μ_i = μ_i° + RT ln a_i. Activity is dimensionless and carries a chosen standard state. For a liquid using the pure-liquid standard at the same T and P, a_i=γ_i x_i. The coefficient γ describes departure from the ideal-solution reference; it is not the activity itself. Comparing coefficients requires the same component order and convention.'),('Residual and excess are different comparisons','A residual property compares a real fluid with the corresponding ideal gas at the same T, P and composition. An excess property compares a real mixture with an ideal solution at the same T, P and composition. EOS calculations and activity models therefore use different reference comparisons. For the chosen liquid convention, gE/(RT)=Σ x_i ln γ_i. Choosing NRTL or Margules is choosing a description of this nonideality.'),('Predict first, infer second','With a model and its parameters, predict pressure and vapor composition. With measured T, P, x and y, invert the ideal-vapor relation to infer activities, then fit model parameters. First check the vapor-pressure equation, logarithm base, units and temperature range. A wrong Psat can be absorbed into fitted parameters and leave a deceptively small residual.')],
 equations=['γ_i = y_i P / [x_i P_i^sat(T)]  (ideal vapor, negligible Poynting correction)','a_i = γ_i x_i;  gE/(RT) = Σ x_i ln γ_i'],
 example='At a synthetic measured point P=100 kPa, x1=0.4 and y1=0.6, choosing Psat1=120 kPa gives γ1=1.25 and a1=0.50. Substituting Psat1=150 kPa instead gives γ1=1.00 and a1=0.40. The mixture measurements have not changed. This calculation isolates the effect of the pure-component property input. It does not show which Psat is physically correct; that requires an applicable source.',
 check='Numerical convergence, thermodynamic consistency and predictive accuracy answer different questions. Inspect residual patterns and model assumptions. An area diagnostic depends on coverage and endpoint treatment. Held-out error uses data excluded from fitting. Neither a low fitting error nor one consistency test certifies the dataset.',
 prompts=['Retrieve Week 1: at 350 K and z1=0.5 with Psat1=150 and Psat2=60 kPa, predict the phases at 80, 95 and 110 kPa. At 95 kPa check z1=(1−0.493506)(0.388889)+0.493506(0.614035).', 'For P=100 kPa, x1=0.4, y1=0.6 and Psat1=120 kPa, show the substitution for γ1 and a1. Explain why activity differs from its coefficient.', 'List the Antoine form, log base, temperature and pressure units and valid range. Could a flexible fit conceal an incorrect Psat?'],
 optional='Choose a controlled consistency defect in Lab 03, a fixed held-out split, or conditional bootstrap spread in Lab 08. Fixed alpha and property assumptions remain outside that bootstrap. Exact synthetic data can produce almost zero spread. No real-data upload is required before class. Use week02-practice.pdf for the bilingual checklist and in-class record; it is the same task, not additional homework.'),
 dict(title='Stable liquid phases and the lever rule',lab='stability',book='11.1-11.2, 14.11, 16.2-16.4',figure='lle',
 question='Why can an equal-fugacity solution still need a stability check?',
 concepts=[('Candidates and stable equilibrium','At fixed T and P, stable equilibrium minimizes total Gibbs energy over admissible states. Equality of chemical potentials identifies candidate coexisting phases. A trivial solution with identical compositions is not a split. A local minimum or a converged equation solver need not identify the globally stable state.'),('The common tangent','For a binary molar mixing-Gibbs curve g(x), two coexisting compositions share a tangent. The tangent must support the entire admitted curve from below. The straight line represents the molar Gibbs energy attainable by mixing amounts of the two phases. Curvature concerns local stability; comparison with all admissible phase combinations concerns global stability.'),('Amounts follow composition','At given coexistence conditions, changing overall composition inside the tie line changes phase amounts while the endpoints stay fixed. This separates the equilibrium calculation from the material-balance calculation. A fit at one temperature does not establish temperature dependence. Replicate measurements of one tie line also do not create additional equilibrium states.')],
 equations=["g′(xα) = g′(xβ) = [g(xβ) − g(xα)]/(xβ − xα)",'fβ = (z1 − xα)/(xβ − xα);  fα = 1 − fβ'],
 example='For a given hypothetical supported tie line xα=0.2 and xβ=0.8, z1=0.5 gives fβ=0.5. If z1 changes to 0.35 at the same coexistence conditions, fβ=0.25 and fα=0.75. Check 0.75(0.2)+0.25(0.8)=0.35. These endpoints are specified for arithmetic, not outputs of the model plotted below.',
 check='The figure illustrates a different synthetic Margules model. Its tie-line endpoints come from the common tangent. Do not copy the numerical endpoints from the hand example into that model. State which evidence came from calculation and which values were supplied.',
 prompts=['Why is a negative local curvature sufficient to reject local stability, while positive curvature alone does not prove global stability?', 'For the hand-example endpoints, predict what changes when z1 moves from 0.5 to 0.35.', 'What would you inspect after a solver reports equal chemical potentials?'],
 optional='Partial molar properties provide a useful bridge: for binary molar m(x), m̄1=m+(1−x)m′ and m̄2=m−xm′. Sketch a tangent and read its intercepts. Verify x m̄1+(1−x)m̄2=m. This is an optional paper exercise, not a new lab. LLE fitting and VLLE are further extensions.'),
 dict(title='Bulk solids, surfaces and vessel constraints',lab='solids',book='16.1, 16.5, 16.8-16.9; compare 17.5',figure='gse',
 question='What fixes the gas pressure when a bulk solid can appear or disappear?',
 concepts=[('Three different gas-solid questions','Bulk solid-vapor equilibrium transfers the same substance between phases, as in sublimation. Adsorption describes uptake at a surface. A gas-solid reaction changes chemical species and needs reaction stoichiometry and an equilibrium constant. Name the process before choosing equations. Lab 10 treats nonreacting bulk equilibrium; Lab 11 treats adsorption. The current reaction-extent solver does not solve general heterogeneous reactions.'),('The vessel matters','For a rigid closed vessel at fixed T and V, equilibrium minimizes Helmholtz energy under the material constraints. For fixed T and P, the corresponding potential is Gibbs energy. In the ideal-gas sublimation model, solid present at equilibrium fixes the subliming species partial pressure at Psub(T). There must be enough total material to fill the gas at that pressure.'),('Depletion and other equilibria','If the available amount is below the saturated gas capacity, the solid disappears and the gas is undersaturated. Inert gas contributes to total pressure in this ideal model. For SLE, identify the solid standard state and liquid activity. A bulk solubility condition and an adsorption loading equation describe different phase inventories.')],
 equations=['n_g,sat = Psub V/(RT);  n_g + n_s = n_total','P = n_total RT/V when the solid is exhausted (no inert gas)'],
 example='Assume a synthetic material at 300 K with Psub=10.0 kPa in a 1.00 L gas volume. Use R=8.314462618 kPa L mol−1 K−1. Saturated gas requires 0.00400908 mol. With 0.0100 mol total, the gas remains at 10.0 kPa and 0.00599092 mol is solid. With only 0.0020 mol, all material is gas and P=4.98868 kPa.',
 check='For a separate Langmuir teaching example, qm=2.00 mmol/g and b=0.100 kPa−1 give q=qm bP/(1+bP)=1.00 mmol/g at 10 kPa. To close a vessel balance, multiply loading by adsorbent mass and convert mmol to mol. This surface amount is not the bulk solid remaining in the sublimation calculation.',
 prompts=['Classify sublimation, adsorption and a gas-solid reaction by what changes physically.', 'Predict pressure when the total amount increases from 0.002 to 0.010 mol in the example.', 'Which thermodynamic potential matches a rigid closed vessel at fixed temperature?'],
 optional='Henry gas-solubility exercise: using the explicitly defined convention p=H x with H=1000 kPa, find x for p=10 kPa. Distinguish this H from a Henry adsorption coefficient. Osmosis exercise: for an ideal dilute nonelectrolyte, estimate Π=cRT at c=10 mol/m³ and T=300 K. These are paper extensions, not implemented new lab modes.'),
 dict(title='Reaction extent before routes to K',lab='reaction',book='17.1-17.3; optional 17.5 and 17.8',figure='reaction',
 question='How does a reaction choose its equilibrium composition?',
 concepts=[('Extent is the composition coordinate','Write n_i=n_i,0+ν_i ξ, with negative stoichiometric coefficients for reactants. Nonnegative species amounts determine the feasible extent interval. Before calculating K, identify the reaction basis, conserved atoms and standard states. The same extent value has different meanings for differently scaled reactions.'),('Driving force along the feasible path','At fixed T and P, dG/dξ=ΔrG=Σν_i μ_i. For an interior equilibrium with the required species present, ΔrG=0. Using dimensionless activities gives ΔrG=ΔrG°+RT ln Q. Thus Q=K at equilibrium and ln K=−ΔrG°/(RT). A boundary minimum needs a one-sided condition; never fabricate a zero-amount logarithm.'),('K comes from thermodynamic information','Once the equilibrium criterion is clear, obtain ΔrG° from direct data, formation properties, or ΔrH°−TΔrS° at the evaluation temperature. Reconcile units and reaction basis before comparing routes. Varying pressure at fixed T changes ideal-gas activities and may change extent, while K stays fixed for the same reaction and standard states. Temperature variation requires applicable thermal data.')],
 equations=['n_i = n_i,0 + ν_i ξ;  dG/dξ = ΔrG° + RT ln Q','Q = Π a_i^ν_i;  ln K = −ΔrG°/(RT)'],
 example='Use the Lab 12 synthetic reaction A2 ⇌ 2A at 350 K, with one initial mole of A2, no A and no inert. Then nA2=1−ξ and nA=2ξ, so 0≤ξ≤1 mol. At P=P°=100 kPa, the numerical extent in moles gives Q=4ξ²/(1−ξ²). With ΔrG°=−4000 J/mol, ln K=1.37454 and K=3.95326. The interior minimum occurs at ξ≈0.70503 mol. Both the Gibbs-energy minimum and Q=K identify the same composition.',
 check='At ξ=0.5 mol, Q=1.33333, below K, so ΔrG is negative and increasing extent lowers G. At ξ=0.9 mol, Q≈17.0526, above K, so the reverse direction lowers G. Doubling pressure at fixed T increases Q at a given extent for this reaction, so equilibrium shifts toward lower extent.',
 prompts=['Write species amounts and the feasible extent interval before using any K formula.', 'At ξ=0.5 mol in the example, predict the favorable direction from Q/K.', 'Why does the pressure effect depend on stoichiometry, while K at fixed T does not?'],
 optional='For multiple reactions, use independent reaction coordinates or minimize Gibbs energy with atom balances. This is reference material beyond the single neutral ideal-gas extent solver. A pure solid may have unit activity while present, but disappearance and reaction feasibility still require checks.'),
 dict(title='Electrochemical equilibrium from reaction thermodynamics',lab='electrochemistry',book='11.11, 14.8-14.9, 17.6',figure='cell',
 question='What determines the sign of a reversible concentration-cell potential?',
 concepts=[('Reaction direction and electrons','Write both half-reactions as reductions. Reverse the left half-reaction and combine it with the right after balancing electrons. Define Ecell=Eright−Eleft. Both standard reduction potentials need the same reference electrode and temperature. Scaling a half-reaction changes amounts and electron count, not its potential.'),('Activities connect chemistry to voltage','Electrochemical potential includes chemical and electrical contributions. For the balanced cell reaction, ΔrG=−nFE and ΔrG°=−nFE°. Combining these with ΔrG=ΔrG°+RT ln Q gives the Nernst equation. Activities are dimensionless and belong to their compartments. Concentration approximates activity only under stated assumptions.'),('A reversible prediction','The calculated potential concerns equilibrium driving force under the chosen conventions. Junction potentials, kinetic losses and resistive drops can affect an actual measured or loaded cell. Keep temperature fixed unless the standard-potential data remain applicable. A reaction quotient from an arbitrary state is not automatically an equilibrium constant.')],
 equations=['E = E° − RT ln Q/(nF);  ΔrG = −nFE','ln K = nFE°/(RT);  R = 8.314462618 J mol−1 K−1'],
 example='For the same M2+/M reduction couple on both sides at 298.15 K, take aLeft=0.0100 and aRight=1.000, n=2 and equal standard potentials. The reaction as written transfers the dissolved species from the right compartment to the left, so Q=aLeft/aRight=0.0100. With E°=0, Eright−Eleft=+0.05915935 V. Using F=96485.33212 C/mol gives ΔrG≈−11.416 kJ per mole of the written cell reaction.',
 check='Exchange the two activities: E reverses sign. Set them equal: E=0. Double the complete reaction basis: n and ΔrG double, while E remains unchanged. This scaling check tests whether the reaction basis and electron count have been used consistently.',
 prompts=['Predict the sign of E before calculating when the right activity exceeds the left.', 'Why can E be nonzero even though E°=0 for this concentration cell?', 'Which quantities change when the complete reaction and electron count are doubled?'],
 optional='Use the activity-model controls only within their stated domains, or relate a fuel-cell equilibrium voltage to reaction Gibbs energy. Separate reversible potential from operating voltage. The supplied table of contents locates fuel-cell voltage in 17.6; the present handout develops the concentration-cell bridge independently.')]
